From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotschmoe <isaac@mass-engineering.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 08:37:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] media: cix: mvx: drive the SCMI perf domain from devfreq

mvx_devfreq_target() calls dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev), and the OPP core
binds the VPU's first clock (vpu_clk), so devfreq moved the clock between
150 MHz and 1.2 GHz while the perf domain the driver attached to stayed at
its lowest level. Find the perf domain's OPP and request its level with
dev_pm_domain_set_performance_state(), and skip requests for the level
already set. dev_pm_opp_set_opp() on the perf domain's virtual device
would also set vpu_clk, since that device shares the VPU's DT node: each
level change then sent a redundant CLOCK_RATE_SET right after the
PERF_LEVEL_SET, and now and then the firmware acked that pair ~15 ms late,
after its delayed response ("Received valid OoO Delayed Response").

mvx_devfreq_get_dev_status(), get_cur_freq() and target() also read the
clock with clk_get_rate(). vpu_clk is an SCMI clock without a rate cache,
so every devfreq poll was a mailbox round trip on the channel the perf
requests share. Report the frequency last set instead.

On a Minisforum MS-R1 the perf domain now follows decode load (level 1200
during decode, 0 before); four parallel 4K decodes (2x VP9, 2x H.264)
ran at ~267 fps with no "Wait pending returned timeout" from the
firmware; SCMI CLOCK_RATE_GET traffic fell from ~34/s at idle to none. Over 90
4K HEVC decodes the VPU made no CLOCK_RATE_SET requests and logged no
out-of-order responses; vpu_clk still follows the level (300 MHz idle,
1.2 GHz decoding) and decode speed is unchanged (~308 fps).

Signed-off-by: hotschmoe <isaac@mass-engineering.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c b/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
@@ -142,6 +142,7 @@
     struct device_link *opp_dl;
     struct devfreq_dev_profile devfreq_profile;
     struct devfreq *devfreq;
+    unsigned long cur_freq;
     unsigned long target_freq;
 };
 
@@ -503,10 +504,32 @@
     }
     dev_pm_opp_put(opp);
 
-    pre_freq = ctx->clk ? clk_get_rate(ctx->clk) : 0;
-    ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, *freq);
-    if (!ret)
+    pre_freq = ctx->cur_freq;
+    if (*freq == pre_freq)
+        return 0;
+
+    /*
+     * dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev) only sets vpu_clk: the OPP core takes the
+     * device's first clock, and the SCMI perf domain (voltage and the
+     * firmware's operating point) stays at its boot level. Request the perf
+     * domain's level instead; the firmware sets the clock with it.
+     *
+     * Not dev_pm_opp_set_opp(opp_pmdomain): the virtual device shares the
+     * VPU's DT node, so its OPP table holds vpu_clk too and every level
+     * change would be followed by a redundant SCMI CLOCK_RATE_SET.
+     */
+    opp = dev_pm_opp_find_freq_exact(ctx->opp_pmdomain, *freq, true);
+    if (IS_ERR(opp)) {
+        dev_err(dev, "No perf domain opp for %lu Hz\n", *freq);
+        return PTR_ERR(opp);
+    }
+    ret = dev_pm_domain_set_performance_state(ctx->opp_pmdomain,
+                                              dev_pm_opp_get_level(opp));
+    dev_pm_opp_put(opp);
+    if (!ret) {
+        ctx->cur_freq = *freq;
         atomic_set(&mvx_log_perf.freq, *freq);
+    }
 
     MVX_LOG_PRINT(&mvx_log_dev, MVX_LOG_DEBUG, "%s() target=%ld, previous=%ld, current=%ld.",
                     __func__, target_freq, pre_freq, *freq);
@@ -518,7 +541,9 @@
 {
     struct mvx_dev_ctx *ctx = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-    *freq = ctx->clk ? clk_get_rate(ctx->clk) : 0;
+    if (!ctx->cur_freq)
+        return -EAGAIN;
+    *freq = ctx->cur_freq;
     MVX_LOG_PRINT(&mvx_log_dev, MVX_LOG_DEBUG, "%s() %ld", __func__, *freq);
 
     return 0;
@@ -530,7 +555,7 @@
     struct mvx_dev_ctx *ctx = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
     update_freq(ctx);
-    stat->current_frequency = ctx->clk ? clk_get_rate(ctx->clk) : 0;
+    stat->current_frequency = ctx->cur_freq;
     stat->busy_time = ctx->target_freq;
     stat->total_time = stat->current_frequency;
 
-- 
2.39.5
